    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [x] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [x] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.icons\n\n### Description\n\nThanks for the convenient `mini.icons` plugin. Here is my desired use case, and why I think adding glob matching would be very useful.\n\nA summarized version of my current config:\n```Lua\nreturn {\n    \"echasnovski/mini.icons\",\n    config = function()\n        require(\"mini.icons\").setup {\n            file = {\n                [\".gitignore\"] = { hl = \"GitIconColor\" },\n                -- other settings\n            },\n            extension = {\n                conf = { glyph = \"\", hl = \"SettingsIconColor\" },\n                -- other settings\n            },\n            -- other settings\n        }\n        MiniIcons.mock_nvim_web_devicons()\n    end,\n}\n```\n\nI'd like all my Hyprland config files to have a different icon. Since Hyprland config files use the `.conf` extension, I'd like to use Glob Matching in the `file` section to specify that only files in the `.config/hypr/` directory should have this specific Hyprland icon.",
    "closed_at": "2025-07-13T17:35:46Z",
