            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for the suggestion!\n\nThe `file` category is specifically designed to match exactly for performance reasons. Having a glob matching is a bit too heavy for performance.\n\nThe Hyprland config files already have out of the box dedicated Neovim filetype (`hyprlang`) and ['mini.icons' icon](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/3f71fd3573981bf18f76b2bfb6518c4ae728638d/lua/mini/icons.lua#L1287). So the use case you describe is intended to be resolved via controlling whether to use data from extension or fall back to filetype. Which is what [`config.use_file_extension`](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/3f71fd3573981bf18f76b2bfb6518c4ae728638d/doc/mini-icons.txt#L257-L289) is for.\n\nSo the setup for Hyprland config can look something like this:\n\n```lua\nrequire('mini.icons').setup({\n  use_file_extension = function(ext, file)\n    if ext ~= 'conf' then return true end\n    -- Use icon from extension only if it is not for Hyprland config\n    return file:find('/hypr/[^/]+%.conf$') == nil\n  end,\n  extension = {\n    conf = { glyph = '', hl = 'SettingsIconColor' },\n    -- other settings\n  },\n  -- other settings\n})\n```\n\nClosing as already possible.",
